Lab 17: Automate OpenStack Deployments with Heat
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Automate multi-tier cloud application deployments using Heat Orchestration Templates (HOT)
Deploy applications with inter-service dependencies using Heat
Implement autoscaling policies for dynamic resource management
Prerequisites
Access to an OpenStack cloud environment with Heat service enabled
OpenStack CLI tools installed (openstack, heat)
Basic understanding of YAML syntax
Familiarity with OpenStack core services (Nova, Neutron, Cinder)
Lab Setup
Environment Verification:

# Verify Heat service is available
openstack orchestration service list
Expected Output: List of Heat engine services with "up" status.

Install Heat Client (if not present):

pip install python-heatclient
Task 1: Create Multi-Tier Application Template
Subtask 1.1: Design Template Structure
Create multi-tier-app.yaml with:

heat_template_version: 2018-08-31
description: Multi-tier web application with database
Subtask 1.2: Define Resources
Add frontend and backend resources:

resources:
  frontend_server:
    type: OS::Nova::Server
    properties:
      image: cirros
      flavor: m1.small
      networks:
        - network: private

  database_server:
    type: OS::Nova::Server
    properties:
      image: cirros
      flavor: m1.medium
      networks:
        - network: private
Subtask 1.3: Add Dependencies
  frontend_floating_ip:
    type: OS::Neutron::FloatingIP
    properties:
      floating_network: public
    depends_on: frontend_server
Key Concept: depends_on ensures proper deployment order.

Task 2: Deploy Application Stack
Subtask 2.1: Validate Template
openstack orchestration template validate -t heat -f multi-tier-app.yaml
Troubleshooting: Fix any YAML syntax errors if validation fails.

Subtask 2.2: Launch Stack
openstack stack create -t multi-tier-app.yaml --wait app-stack
Subtask 2.3: Verify Deployment
openstack stack list
openstack server list
Expected Outcome: Stack in CREATE_COMPLETE state with two running instances.

Task 3: Implement Autoscaling
Subtask 3.1: Create Scaling Policy
Add to multi-tier-app.yaml:

  scaling_policy:
    type: OS::Heat::ScalingPolicy
    properties:
      adjustment_type: change_in_capacity
      scaling_adjustment: 1
      cooldown: 60
      resource: frontend_group
Subtask 3.2: Define Scaling Group
  frontend_group:
    type: OS::Heat::AutoScalingGroup
    properties:
      min_size: 1
      max_size: 3
      resource:
        type: OS::Nova::Server
        properties:
          # Same as frontend_server properties
Subtask 3.3: Trigger Scaling
# Manually trigger scaling (simulate alarm)
openstack stack update --existing -t multi-tier-app.yaml app-stack
Monitoring: Use openstack stack resource list app-stack to watch scaling.

Conclusion
In this lab you have:

Created a Heat Orchestration Template for multi-tier applications
Deployed interdependent cloud resources with proper sequencing
Implemented auto-scaling policies for dynamic workloads
Verification:

openstack stack output show app-stack all
Cleanup:

openstack stack delete app-stack
